\details{
\subsection{Naming conventions (\code{study})}{

CATLAS uses a specific study naming convention that allows it to keep some
order in the chaos that is scientific publishing. Each experiment must be
named according to this convention, and thus be of the form
\verb{<prefix>-<task>-<mode>-0X}. In this study name, each components carries its
own specific meaning:
\itemize{
\item \verb{<prefix>} means study type or manuscript family. For instance, \code{fba}
refers to \code{forward-blocking-attention} experiments aimed at illuminating
the role attention plays in the forward blocking phenomenon in cue
contingency learning.
\item \verb{<task>} is a broad family to which task used by the experiment falls. It
refers to the cover story, not to the formal structure of the experiment.
For instance, \code{allergy} refers to the commonly used allergy prediction
family of tasks.
\item \verb{<mode>} refers to the data collection mode. Currently support online
(\code{"online"}), behavioural on-site (\code{"lab"}), eye-tracking containing
(\code{"eye"}), and electroencephalography containing (\code{"eeg"}) data.
\item \verb{0X} is the wave indicating order of this experiment in a series of
experiments from the same study type/manuscript family (\verb{<prefix>}), using
the same task variant (\verb{<task>}), and collected via the same data
collection method (\verb{<mode>}). The order does NOT reset with each new
manuscript and it is thus possible to have the first experiment of a
manuscript containing wave number of more than one. The wave identifier
\verb{0X} is coerced to integer via \code{as.integer()}.
}
}

\subsection{Supported study designs (\code{design})}{

Study \code{design} parameter provides information about above and beyond what you
put into \code{study}. Most importantly, while the \verb{<task>} field of \code{study}
informs about the experiment's "cover story," \code{design} reflects formal
set-up and study procedure information. Currently, the following designs are
supported:
\itemize{
\item \code{three-stage-replication} refers to a formally exact replication of the
Beesley & Le Pelley's (2011) study which demonstrated that forward blocking
in a three-stage cue-outcome contingency learning paradigm correlates with
eye-tracking proxies of overt attention
\item \code{three-stage-extension} refers to a procedure reflecting Paskewitz &
Jones's (2020) desing suggestion for adjudicating between attentional shift
and attentional competition accounts of forward blocking within
associability-based associative models of cue-outcome contingency learning
}
}
}
